      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "This is to assess one of the rare complications after total knee replacement and to assess risk factors of failure."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "11 patients with varus knee and an average age of 67 years underwent TKA between 2005 and 2013. All patients returned with a sudden sharp knee pain, disability to walk and significant decrease in ROM about 4 to 8 weeks after surgery. Radiographic examination revealed a lateral femoral condylar stress fracture."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "After analyzing the images, we found common characteristics among all patients, which might be attributable to the later fracture including varus deformity>25, femoral component lateralization, and valgus correction."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Surgeons should be aware of the risk factors to consider before, during, and after surgery."
        }
      ]},
